nss_qdisc: keep a codel leaf buildable when its flow queue memory is scarce

nssfq_codel hands the firmware one physically contiguous block for its whole
flow queue array. At the default 1024 flows that is 128 KB, an order-5
allocation, and orders above PAGE_ALLOC_COSTLY_ORDER are failed outright
rather than compacted for, so a router that has been forwarding for a while
refuses it. The qdisc went down with the allocation, which is not a survivable
outcome here: until some leaf below the root carries set_default, the shaper
tree drops every frame the interface is asked to transmit - ARP and PPPoE
discovery included - so a single refused allocation took the port silently off
the air, carrier up and nothing logged, until the next reboot. Halve the flow
count until it fits instead, and ask the allocator to work for it first.

The same function could not build a flow-queue codel at all unless the count
was spelled out. A zero count from tc means "use the default" on nssfq_codel
and "single queue" only on plain nsscodel, but the firmware size request was
gated on the value rather than on the qdisc kind, so the per-flow size stayed
zero and the allocation asked for get_order(0) - BITS_PER_LONG minus
PAGE_SHIFT, an order nothing can satisfy. Every `tc qdisc add ... nssfq_codel`
without an explicit `flows` therefore failed, and left the same dark tree
behind. Resolve the effective count in one place so the size request, the
allocation and the firmware message all agree on it, and keep the requested
count so a later change command that repeats the original request is not
rejected by a fallback the user never asked for.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

